o tumour however  the proto Murs do not have identical  three-dimensional confirmations let's go  back to our original color scheme we can  see the conformational difference in the  pro tumors by examining a section of the  spike protein that is critical to the  viral life cycle the receptor binding  domain or RB d the RB D is where this  virus binds to an enzyme on the host  cells surface enabling it to fuse with  the cell and transport viral genetic  material inside two of these R biddies  are in a Down conformation in the  structure let's color these yellow  however one of these are B DS is flipped  up this up conformation is higher energy  poised to bind to the cellular receptor  and result in fusion it's thought that  when the spike protein binds each of  these are b DS shifts into this less  stable up conformation our own peptide  bond breaking enzymes called proteases  can cut the spike protein at specific  sites and conformational changes in the  spike protein enable fusion to occur a  different research group published a  crystal structure of just the receptor  binding domain of the spike protein will  keep the RB d colored white in this  structure the RB D is bound to  angiotensin converting enzyme 2 also  called ace 2 which is the receptor on  our cell surface that the corona virus  binds to cause fusion notice these  structures are heavily glycosylated as  well let's hide the sugars in this model  so we can focus in on some of the weak  binding interactions that hold the RB d  and ace 2 together for example we have  an extensive hydrogen bonding network at  the RB d  a Stu interface involving these two  tyrosine residues this tyrosine  sidechain is also hydrogen bonded to the  carbonyl of this asparagine sidechain  which in turn bonds through its NH  hydrogen atom to the carbonyl of this  glutamine on a Stu moving along the ACE  to alpha helix we have a glutamate side  chain which is deprotonated at pH 7.4  and a lysine residue that carries a  positive charge at that pH these  residues are both involved in hydrogen  bonding with the Amadou of a glutamine  sidechain notice that these hydrogen  bonds are relatively short owing to the  strong hydrogen bonds that can form when  charged amino acid side chains are  involved in this region we have  extensive hydrogen bonding we see some  between amino acid backbone atoms and  some additional hydrogen bonds among  polar and charged residues like the  hydrogen bonds of this tyrosine with a  negatively charged glutamate side chain  and a positively charged arginine  sidechain overall characterization of  the spike protein is super important  because this paves the way for vaccine  development if we're able to prime our  immune system by introducing small  harmless peptides that resemble the  spike protein our immune system can  recognize and build up antibodies  against it protecting us against Kovan  19 infection in the future also knowing  what the RB d binds to could help us  develop treatments that tie up the virus  by binding it outside of the cell before  it's able to fuse and cause infection if  the virus does undergo fusion viral  genetic material is released into the  cell in the case of corona viruses this  piece of RNA travels to our own cells  ribosome and hijacks them to create its  own viral proteins an interesting thing  I learned making this video is that this  viral RNA is capable of shifting the  three-letter frame of RNA bases that's  read by the ribosome this essentially  doubles the peptide sequences that can  be made from one single strand of viral  RNA viral translation using our  ribosomes makes the proteins that the  virus needs to assemble additional  copies of itself which will eventually  be released from the cell and infect  other cells there's an important protein  that's translated in this process and  that's the main protease that cleaves  the viral polypeptide chain into the  functional proteins needed to assemble  new viruses this is another therapeutic  target if an individual is already  infected with the virus but you can  administer a drug that binds to the  protease and stops it from creating  mature viral proteins viral replication  can be slowed this main Tsarskoe of two  protease is a dimer made up of two  identical protein chains and it must  dimerize to become a functional protease  there are many amino acid interactions  at the dimer interface but the  researchers that publish this crystal  structure suggests that the ionic  interactions between the sidechain of  this arginine residue and this glutamate  drive the dimerization this interaction  is present on both sides of the dimer  moving over to the active site the  important residues are this cysteine  side chain and this histidine this  enzyme is a cysteine protease so it uses  the nucleophilic cysteine to attack the  amat bond of a peptide in the mechanism  the histidine nitrogen grabs the proton  off cysteine side chain allowing it to  attack the peptide bond the peptide bond  is cleaved and then a water molecule can  come in releasing cysteine so that the  protease can cleave another polypeptide  enzymes containing nucleophilic  catalytic residues are excellent targets  for irreversible inhibition because they  contain a nucleophilic amino acid side  chain cysteine in this case inhibitors  can be designed that bind to the enzyme  with a permanent covalent bond unlike  reversible inhibitors that can pop in  and out of an active site  these irreversible also called suicide  inhibitors permanently inactivate the  enzyme preventing it from doing its job  and creating more viral proteins these  researchers had previously designed  inhibitors for other coronavirus  proteases they were able to bind one of  these inhibitors to the active site of  the Czar's co2 protease and that's what  we're looking at here  notice this cysteine is clearly involved  in a covalent bond with the ketone of  the inhibitor now this is a reversible  reaction so this isn't a suicide  inhibitor itself but I still think it's  pretty neat to see that cysteine tied up  in a covalent bond in this structure  over here this carbonyl of the inhibitor  is making hydrogen bonds with three  backbone NH groups of the protein the  catalytic histidine of the protease is  also involved in a hydrogen bond  this ring over here is involved in an  extensive hydrogen bonding Network  involving both backbone atoms and side  chains knowing the contacts an inhibitor  makes with an enzyme allows chemists and  biologists to consider the interactions  and potentially design even better  inhibitors because kovat 19 infection is  particularly harmful to the lungs this  research group administered the  inhibitor directly into the lungs of  mice by inhalation the mice did not  suffer adverse effects from this  treatment and so this could potentially  lead to promising Cova 19 treatments in  the future every new piece of  information we learn about viruses leads  us to a better understanding of disease  and treatment you might be wondering  what you can do at a time like this  first of all if videos like this  interests you keep at it work on your  understanding of disease and structure  so that you can be part of the cure in  the future  follow the hygiene and social distancing  guidelines not only to prevent getting  the disease but to slow the spread of  Kovan 19 so that we can keep our  researchers and the health care workers  on the frontlines safe and be sure to  take care of your own mental health  reach out to friends and family by phone  text video chat spend time on your  hobbies get some exercise we're going to  need that awesome brain of yours in the  future so take good care of it
 

